{:edb/db-before-t 2710 :edb/db-after-t 2711 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536023 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:06:02.710Z" 13194139536023 true] [17592186164155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164159 13194139536023 true] [17592186164160 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164163 13194139536023 true] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379797 13194139536023 true] [17592186166843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379804 13194139536023 true] [17592186166843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379811 13194139536023 true] [17592186167132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536023 true] [17592186167132 1100 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" 13194139536023 true] [17592186167132 1100 "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, x(t) = e^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} + e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t = \\ln 2$?" 13194139536023 false] [17592186167132 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{59}{4})-2(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ 2(\\frac{59}{4})-3(\\frac{11}{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 true] [17592186167133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536023 true] [17592186167133 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139536023 true] [17592186167133 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139536023 false] [17592186167133 1145 "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/6ba5066fd4ddc43491c3d007355e0b64c84d449d6462812aa4c8ca0450887a8b.png)" 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379798 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1089 "q-332627" 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1100 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1113 #uuid "556ea4da-7963-5f2a-ac1a-0192abc563d1" 13194139536023 true] [17592186379797 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536023 true] [17592186379798 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379799 13194139536023 true] [17592186379798 1000 #edb/ref 175